# Line-ending policy. Added 2026-08-09 after a deployment audit, and deliberately
# NARROW: the first version of this file said `* text=auto eol=lf`, which renormalised
# fourteen files -- among them reports/methods_and_limitations_v1.md,
# scripts/legacy/qwen_max_activating_tokens.json, reports/adjudication_merged.json,
# reports/adjudication_ledger_r2.canonical.json and results/gemma3_sweep/analysis/
# sweep_analysis.json. Their content was untouched; only line endings changed. But
# VERIFICATION_LOG.md records 84 sha256 digests and names two of those files directly,
# and in this project the digest IS the artifact's identity -- a hash-bound file whose
# bytes change has lost the thing that binds it, whether or not a human can see a
# difference. A tidy-up rule would have silently invalidated the provenance chain that
# the whole adjudication discipline rests on. It was reverted before it was committed.
#
# So the default here is the opposite of tidiness: freeze the bytes.
* -text

# The one exception, and it is preventive rather than corrective. An audit flagged
# slurm/launch_certify.sh as CRLF; on inspection that was true only of the Windows
# working copy (2140 bytes, mixed 31 CRLF / 17 LF). The committed blob is clean LF at
# 2109 bytes, shebang `#!/bin/bash` with no trailing \r, so every cluster checkout has
# always received a runnable script and nothing was ever broken. The rule stays anyway:
# a CRLF shebang makes Linux report "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M", an error that names
# the interpreter rather than the file and so reads as a broken environment rather than
# a broken script. This pins .sh to LF in the repository so a Windows-side working copy
# can never commit that failure into a blob. No .sh here is hash-bound, so the rule
# costs nothing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Gitignored today, but declare them binary so a future force-add cannot be mangled.
*.pt binary
*.safetensors binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
